Name the bad catalog colour entry, without a trace that changes the failure rate.

Qt dies at a MEASURED 3 runs in 8 (#227) inside a named colour conversion, and the receiver of
the inner send is not a colour: O2Color one run, __NSCFString the next, which is freed memory
rather than a wrong type. The obvious instrument, a trace per colour lookup, is exactly the one
that cannot be used: with CIDER_WAYLAND_TRACE_COLORS on the rate goes to 0 in 8. The trace
destroys what it measures.

So these ask a question instead of printing an answer. respondsToSelector is cheap and SILENT
while the entry is sound, and the only run that writes anything is the one that was about to die,
which is the run whose name nobody could read.

TWO OF THEM, placed to decide one fork that was otherwise undecidable: was the entry already
wrong when it was stored, or did it go wrong afterwards.

MEASURED, over 8 runs ending in a death:
  BAD ON ARRIVAL   0 times, in every survivor and in the death
  BAD ENTRY at use  fires, name=controlTextColor catalog=System class=O2Color

So the entry is a VALID COLOUR WHEN STORED and is spoiled later. The resolver is exonerated: this
is a lifetime fault on _color, not a wrong object being handed in.

WHAT THAT ELIMINATES, so the next reader does not redo it: colors.rs returns nil rather than the
name for an unknown colour; NSColor copyWithZone is "return [self retain]", so _color genuinely
retains; controlTextColor resolves through Recipe::ClassMethod("blackColor") and blackColor
returns a FRESH autoreleased colour each call rather than a shared singleton, so there is no
obvious singleton to over-release. Something releases a colour it does not own.

